  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1220 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nEarly this Monday morning, isolated showers and widespread \ncloud cover were observed ahead of an upper level trough located\nover southwest Kansas. Additionally, another upper level trough\nwas situated over northern Colorado with another upstream upper\nwave appearing on water vapor loop near southern Nevada. These \nupper waves were rotating around a 500mb high located over the \nsouthwest United States. At the surface, a trough of low \npressure extended across far western Kansas. Models this morning\nover the next 12 to 24 hours were in good agreement that our \nnext upper wave will progress into the West Central High Plains \nlate today as the surface boundary shifts westward. By early \nevening scattered convection is expected to develop once again \nacross eastern Colorado, similar to recent nights, and then \ntrack eastward into western Kansas during the overnight hours. \nThe main risk from these storms overnight will be wind gusts of \n50 mph higher. \n\nA better chance for thunderstorms will occur on Tuesday as the \nnext in a series of upper level troughs approach western Kansas.\nModels appears to have a decent handle on the track of this \nupper wave that was located near southern Nevada earlier today. \nLarge hail and strong gusty winds will be possible from these \nstorms on Tuesday given the improving instability and lift \nforecast late Tuesday as thunderstorms develop across eastern \nColorado. These storm may increase in coverage as they move \nacross western Kansas along a instability/0-1km theta-e ridge \naxis overnight. If these storms cluster together \novernight...heavy rainfall will be a risk Tuesday night given \nthe high PWATs (around 1.5 inches) forecast across the area. If \nthis does occur a few areas could have ponding of water in poor \ndrainage area and secondary roads. \n\n\nThe chance of thunderstorms will then persist on Wednesday as a\nsurface cold front crosses southwest Kansas and additional \nupper waves embedded in the northwest flow cross western Kansas.\nAlso following the frontal passage there will be some cooler, \nmore seasonal, air filtering into the area during the day and \ngiven this cooler air spreading into western Kansas the highs \nmidweek will on average between 5 and 10 degrees cooler than \nwhat we currently expect today(Monday). Highs on Wednesday will \nbe mainly in the lower 90s with high only in the upper 80s being\npossible around Garden City and along the I-70 corridor. \n\nThese cooler temperatures midweek however will be brief. By \nlate week the upper high over the southwest United States will \nbegin to build east towards the Rockies and based on the 850mb \ntemperature trends late week into the upcoming weekend it \nappears that the high temperatures will be warming through mid \nto upper the 90s late week with 100 degree temperatures \nreturning during the weekend period.",
